10:46:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand why that sounds like “this is your fault” or “you should be able to stop it.” But medically, **being told to calm down or slow your breathing does not mean the person is in control of the asthma attack itself.** Clinicians may say it because panic and rapid, shallow breathing can add a second problem on top of the airway narrowing: - Fast breathing can make it harder to fully breathe out. - In asthma, exhaling is already obstructed, so rapid breaths can trap more air in the lungs and worsen chest tightness. - Fear can make someone tense their chest, throat, and shoulders, increasing the sensation of breathlessness. So coaching someone to sit upright, try not to gasp, and take slower breaths—often with longer, gentler exhalation—may help them **use what airway they still have more efficiently** while medication works. But it cannot “will away” the underlying bronchospasm, swelling, and mucus. Someone can be calm, skilled at breathing exercises, and still have a severe attack needing a rescue inhaler, steroids, oxygen, or emergency care. Sometimes healthcare workers also have difficulty distinguishing asthma from things such as panic attacks, hyperventilation, or vocal-cord dysfunction, which *can* respond more directly to breathing techniques. But telling a person with genuine asthma to “just calm down” without assessing them or treating the airway problem is dismissive and potentially dangerous. A better message would be: **“You’re having trouble because your airways are narrowed. You’re not causing this. Let’s use your rescue treatment; I’ll help you slow your breathing while it takes effect.”**
