10:46:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A doctor may reasonably recommend Buteyko-style breathing as an **adjunct** for some people with asthma. But that does not prove that asthma attacks are fully under conscious voluntary control. Research on breathing exercises, including Buteyko, suggests they can help some people with: - perceived breathlessness and anxiety about breathing - symptom scores and quality of life - over-breathing/hyperventilation patterns - sometimes reduced use of reliever inhalers But studies have generally **not shown reliable improvement in the underlying airway inflammation or lung function**, and they have not established that breathing exercises alone can safely stop significant asthma attacks or replace prescribed controller/rescue medication. That distinction matters. Breathing can influence the body’s automatic systems indirectly—heart rate is a good example: you cannot command your heart to beat at exactly 62 beats per minute, but slow breathing can often lower it somewhat. Likewise, breathing exercises may reduce panic, avoid rapid shallow breaths and air trapping, relax upper-airway tension, and possibly change autonomic signals enough to help mild symptoms. But that is not the same as consciously opening inflamed, mucus-filled, bronchospastic airways on command. It is also possible that some people labeled as having “asthma attacks” have a mixture of asthma and other problems—such as hyperventilation, dysfunctional breathing, or inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal-cord/throat narrowing). Those can improve dramatically with breathing training, which may make it seem as though all the symptoms were directly controllable asthma. So your aunt’s experience and Buteyko can both be valid: breathing training may help her catch mild symptoms early and prevent escalation. The unsafe leap is concluding that **anyone can stop any asthma attack through focus alone**, or that needing an inhaler means they failed to control themselves. For someone with asthma, breathing techniques should fit into an asthma action plan, not replace it. Severe or worsening symptoms still need rescue medication and urgent medical assessment when indicated.
