10:49:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. People with asthma are not helpless passengers, and they can become very skilled at reading and managing their bodies. They may have a lot of practical control over outcomes by noticing early warning signs, avoiding triggers, using a controller inhaler consistently if prescribed, taking a reliever inhaler early when their plan says to, pacing activity, using breathing skills, and seeking help before symptoms become severe. Being in tune with symptoms is valuable. What they do not have is guaranteed conscious command over every biological process involved in an attack. That is true of everyone: we can influence our bodies substantially without being able to directly will every process on or off. A fair summary is: - **Agency:** real and important. - **Breathing and calming skills:** useful, sometimes enough for mild symptoms. - **Asthma physiology:** can still overpower those skills, especially in a severe flare. - **Medication and emergency care:** tools that increase control; not evidence of personal failure. That view supports self-management without implying that someone who needs treatment simply was not “in tune” enough.
