10:50:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may help to separate **“not directly controlling every process”** from **“having no control over your body.”** You do not consciously run each heartbeat, digestive enzyme, immune cell, or airway muscle contraction—and that is actually what lets you live while sleeping, concentrating, or unconscious. Your body has automatic systems that keep working without needing your moment-by-moment permission. But you are still very much an active part of the system: - You can choose activity, rest, food, sleep, and environments. - You can influence heart rate and digestion through breathing, exercise, stress management, and habits. - You can notice symptoms and respond early. - You can use treatments that change what the body is doing. So it is not “your body runs the show without you.” It is more like: **you are the person making choices within a body that also has highly capable automatic systems.** You direct some functions directly, influence many others, and rely on automatic regulation for the rest. With asthma, that means you can have real agency—breathing skills, trigger avoidance, medication, an action plan—without having to carry the impossible burden of personally commanding every airway muscle to behave.
