10:51:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That observation may be real: slowing down and focusing on breathing can genuinely improve how someone feels and may help a mild episode settle. It can reduce rapid shallow breathing, chest and throat tension, and the fear-driven “I can’t get air” spiral. But seeing improvement after calming down does not by itself show that calming was the only cause. Often several things happen together: - They stop exercising or leave the trigger. - They sit upright and rest. - They use an inhaler, which can begin helping within minutes. - The flare was mild and was going to settle. - Some of the symptoms may be from hyperventilation or throat tightening as well as asthma. Also, someone can sound and feel better while airway narrowing is still present. That is why clinicians use things like symptoms, peak-flow readings, oxygen level, and response to medication—not just whether someone looks calmer. So the fair conclusion from what you’ve seen is: **calming and slow breathing can be an important part of improvement and sometimes may be enough for mild symptoms.** It does not establish that medication is irrelevant or that a more serious asthma attack can safely be treated by breathing control alone.
