10:52:23 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
10:52:23 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=6944 miss=241
10:52:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Swimmers and singers absolutely can train impressive control over **breathing mechanics**: - when they inhale and exhale - how slowly or deeply they breathe - breath-holding and tolerance of rising carbon dioxide - posture and chest/diaphragm coordination - efficiency of respiratory muscles That training can help them feel less breathless and may help some people manage mild asthma symptoms more effectively. But it does not give direct conscious control over the airway changes specific to asthma: tightening of airway smooth muscle, inflammation/swelling of the lining, and mucus. A trained swimmer can still have exercise-induced asthma; many elite athletes do, and they use asthma medication when needed. Also, adults generally do not train their lungs into being physically much larger. What improves is mostly technique, muscle conditioning, and tolerance/efficiency—not the ability to consciously open inflamed small airways. So asthma is not “different” in the sense that breathing training is useless—it can be helpful. It is different because an asthma flare adds an airway-obstruction problem that skill alone cannot reliably override.
